        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"verona-colei-che-non-si-deve-amare-5aa8ee516ad6c8d8\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Rafa’s repeated “Ti amo” primarily avows as true his loving desire for Loretta. Its persuasive courtship setting gives it a slight commitment-invoking undertone, but the words do not independently constitute a clear undertaking. Although delivered passionately, they form part of an extended, purposeful argument rather than a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is slight uncertainty over P: the declaration supports Rafa’s proposal and implies intended exclusivity, but reassurance, courtship, and future planning do not by themselves establish strong performative force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Rafa directly tells Loretta “Ti amo,” immediately repeating the declaration within a face-to-face plea.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Rafa uses the avowal to answer Loretta’s suspicion that she is merely one in a succession of mistresses and to persuade her to accept his proposed arrangement. His claims that she is his first real love and that he would change houses reinforce an assertion of singular attachment. The surrounding supplication, desire, and subsequent physical coercion complicate the scene and may cast doubt on sincerity or make the declaration instrumentally persuasive, but these factors do not alter its core truth-presenting mode or independently establish E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Non ho avuto nessun amore, prima di conoscere te. Ora tu mi sembri la prima. Ti amo, ti amo in tutti i modi.”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration explicitly presents Rafa’s love as a current emotional state and frames Loretta as uniquely significant, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, Loretta asks how many mistresses have occupied the chair; Rafa denies that earlier women were lovers and says, “Non erano te.”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance functions as deliberate reassurance and persuasion in response to a challenge, rather than as an uncontrolled verbal reflex.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rafa has proposed a villa, travel, and other provisions, then begs Loretta to say yes; the narration describes passionate eyes and a pleading voice.\",\"supports\":\"The broader courtship proposal supplies limited P pressure, while the explicitly passionate delivery alone does not establish E.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as affirmative present-tense Italian “ti amo” in a main-or-embedded syntactic family and retained as a valid explicit I-love-you instance.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted occurrence is an explicit present love declaration rather than a merely related expression.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the utterance’s dominant avowal, with a small allowance for commitment-invoking courtship force under P. Passion, strategic persuasion, and coercive context can be described contextually without E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
